# §7b witness (book §12, the for/print trace; a call in print's place, made in the last iteration because `for\j`
# after `end: for` is not resolved yet): the working acc is 9 and the for body's working j is 9, both dirty, and
# nothing has published them.  `for\j` is an explicit graph read, not a checkpoint: it reads the published 0.  The
# actual arguments are evaluated before the pre-call publication, so take gets 9 and 0 -- 90.  After the call
# `for\j` reads the published 9.  Under writes in place take would get 9 and 9 -- 99.
fn: take (int: a; int: b) int
    return: a * 10 + b
end: take

fn: test () int
    int: acc
    acc: 0
    int: r 0
    int: after 0
    for: int(i, 0) (i < 10) i++
        int: j i
        acc: j
        if: i = 9
            r: take(acc; for\j)
            after: for\j
        ---
    end: for
    if: after != 9
        return: 1
    return: r
end: test

sendMessage: exit(exit_code: test(); stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
